(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze subject-verb agreement rules when subjects are modified by parenthetical prepositional phrases or quasi-conjunctions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Core Grammatical Principle:) Tj
T*
(   The grammatical number of a subject noun is never altered by intervening prepositional phrases,) Tj
T*
(   parenthetical expressions, or quasi-connectors:) Tj
T*
(   - Quasi-connectors include: as well as, along with, together with, in addition to, accompanied) Tj
T*
(   by, including, except, besides.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Diagnostic Case Studies in Biotechnology:) Tj
T*
(   - Example 1: "The lead investigator, along with three postdoctoral researchers, [is / are]) Tj
T*
(   attending the genomics symposium.") Tj
T*
(   - Subject: 'The lead investigator' \(singular\). The parenthetical phrase 'along with...' is) Tj
T*
(   non-restrictive.) Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The lead investigator, along with three postdoctoral researchers, is attending...") Tj
T*
(   - Example 2: "The gene expression pattern, in addition to protein abundance levels, [correlates) Tj
T*
(   / correlate] with clinical outcome.") Tj
T*
(   - Subject: 'The gene expression pattern' \(singular\).) Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "...correlates with clinical outcome.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Plural Compound Subject Exception:) Tj
T*
(   Only the true coordinating conjunction 'and' creates a compound plural subject requiring a) Tj
T*
(   plural verb:) Tj
T*
(   - "The lead investigator and three postdoctoral researchers are attending...") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 8:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine subject-verb concord with correlative conjunctions \(either...or, neither...nor, not only...but also\) and the Rule of Proximity.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Principle of Proximity \(Attraction\):) Tj
T*
(   When subjects are joined by disjunctive or correlative conjunctions \('either...or',) Tj
T*
(   'neither...nor', 'not only...but also'\), the finite verb agrees in person and number strictly) Tj
T*
(   with the closer subject constituent \(the noun phrase immediately adjacent to the verb\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Applied Scientific Scenarios:) Tj
T*
(   - Singular Closer Constituent:) Tj
T*
(   - "Neither the multi-channel pipettes nor the spectrophotometer [was / were] properly) Tj
T*
(   calibrated.") Tj
T*
(   - Closer subject: 'the spectrophotometer' \(singular\).) Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "Neither the multi-channel pipettes nor the spectrophotometer was properly) Tj
T*
(   calibrated.") Tj
T*
(   - Plural Closer Constituent:) Tj
T*
(   - "Neither the spectrophotometer nor the multi-channel pipettes [was / were] properly) Tj
T*
(   calibrated.") Tj
T*
(   - Closer subject: 'the multi-channel pipettes' \(plural\).) Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "Neither the spectrophotometer nor the multi-channel pipettes were properly) Tj
T*
(   calibrated.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Stylistic Best Practice:) Tj
T*
(   To avoid awkward-sounding sentences when one element is singular and the other plural, place) Tj
T*
(   the plural constituent second, immediately preceding the plural verb.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

