(   Object, Predicate Nominative, or Prepositional Complement:) Tj
T*
(   - As Subject: "How the pathogen evades immune recognition remains an open empirical question.") Tj
T*
(   - As Direct Object: "The clinical study proved that the recombinant vaccine elicited) Tj
T*
(   neutralizing antibodies.") Tj
T*
(   - As Prepositional Complement: "The results depend on whether the transcription factor binds) Tj
T*
(   the promoter.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Noun Clauses vs. Restrictive Relative Clauses:) Tj
T*
(   - Noun Complement Clause: Introduces the content of an abstract noun \(e.g., fact, hypothesis,) Tj
T*
(   finding, idea\). 'That' is a conjunction, not a pronoun:) Tj
T*
(   - "The hypothesis that telomerase prevents cellular senescence was supported." \(No comma\).) Tj
T*
(   - Relative Clause: Modifies an antecedent noun where 'that' functions as a relative pronoun) Tj
T*
(   replacing the noun:) Tj
T*
(   - "The hypothesis that was formulated by Dr. Watson was supported.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Punctuation Rule:) Tj
T*
(   Restrictive noun clauses and complement clauses are never separated from their governing verbs) Tj
T*
(   or nouns by commas.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
