/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 14:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze adverbial clauses of condition, concession, and cause, focusing on punctuation conventions and register.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Adverbial Clause Classification:) Tj
T*
(   - Condition: if, unless, provided that, in the event that.) Tj
T*
(   - "Unless the cell density exceeds 80% confluence, do not initiate transfection.") Tj
T*
(   - Concession: although, even though, whereas, while.) Tj
T*
(   - "Although the recombinant strain exhibited slow growth kinetics, its protein yield per cell) Tj
T*
(   was exceptional.") Tj
T*
(   - Cause: because, since, as, given that.) Tj
T*
(   - "Because the primary antibody was degraded, no chemiluminescent signal was detected.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Punctuation Norms Based on Clause Position:) Tj
T*
(   - Sentence-Initial Adverbial Clause: Must be followed by a comma to demarcate the clause) Tj
T*
(   boundary and signal the impending independent clause:) Tj
T*
(   - "Since the incubator temperature fluctuated wildly, the cell morphology altered.") Tj
T*
(   - Sentence-Final Adverbial Clause: Generally requires no comma when restrictive \(essential to) Tj
T*
(   condition or causality\):) Tj
T*
(   - "The cell morphology altered because the incubator temperature fluctuated wildly.") Tj
T*
(   - Exception: Concessive clauses with 'whereas' or non-restrictive 'although' retain a preceding) Tj
T*
(   comma even in terminal position:) Tj
T*
(   - "The yield was high in flask A, whereas flask B showed complete inhibition.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
