BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the grammatical problem of faulty predication in technical writing, illustrating errors and clear corrections.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition of Faulty Predication:) Tj
T*
(   Faulty predication occurs when the subject and the predicate verb/complement of a sentence do) Tj
T*
(   not make logical or ontological sense together. The grammatical structure forces a pairing) Tj
T*
(   between incompatible semantic concepts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Common Categories of Faulty Predication:) Tj
T*
(   - "The reason is because...") Tj
T*
(   - Flawed: "The reason for the contamination is because the filter was torn.") Tj
T*
(   - Analysis: 'The reason' is an abstract noun of causation; 'because' is an adverbial) Tj
T*
(   conjunction. They are redundantly mismatched.) Tj
T*
(   - Correction: "The reason for the contamination is that the filter was torn" OR "The culture) Tj
T*
(   was contaminated because the filter was torn.") Tj
T*
(   - "The purpose is when..." / "...is where...") Tj
T*
(   - Flawed: "Electrophoresis is where molecules are separated by electrical charge.") Tj
T*
(   - Analysis: Electrophoresis is a physical process, not a physical location \('where'\).) Tj
T*
(   - Correction: "Electrophoresis is a laboratory technique in which molecules are separated by) Tj
T*
(   electrical charge.") Tj
T*
(   - Incompatible Subject-Verb Actions:) Tj
T*
(   - Flawed: "The objective of the experiment determined enzyme activity.") Tj
T*
(   - Analysis: An 'objective' cannot determine anything; a researcher determines things.) Tj
T*
(   - Correction: "The objective of the experiment was to determine enzyme activity.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 19:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze inverted conditional sentences \(omission of 'if' with had, were, should\) in formal scientific hypotheses.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Conditional Inversion Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   In high-register academic English, conditional clauses introduced by 'if' can be made more) Tj
T*
(   concise and formal by omitting 'if' and inverting the auxiliary verb with the subject:) Tj
T*
(   - Had + Subject + Past Participle \(Third Conditional / Past Unreal\):) Tj
T*
(   - Standard: "If the temperature had exceeded 42 deg C, the enzyme would have denatured.") Tj
T*
(   - Inverted: "Had the temperature exceeded 42 deg C, the enzyme would have denatured.") Tj
T*
(   - Were + Subject + Infinitive \(Second Conditional / Present/Future Unreal\):) Tj
T*
(   - Standard: "If the concentration were to drop below 5 mM, crystallization would cease.") Tj
T*
(   - Inverted: "Were the concentration to drop below 5 mM, crystallization would cease.") Tj
T*
(   - Should + Subject + Base Verb \(First Conditional / Low Probability\):) Tj
T*
(   - Standard: "If contamination occur, autoclave the entire batch immediately.") Tj
T*
ET

